      "index": 0,
      "content": "Dijkstra\u2019s algorithm fails with negative edge weights because it greedily selects the node with the smallest *known* distance and assumes this distance is final\u2014never revising it. With negative edges, a shorter path to an already \u201csettled\u201d node may appear later, but Dijkstra ignores it.\n\n**Example (3 nodes):**  \nNodes: *A* (source), *B*, *C*.  \nEdges: *A\u2192B* (weight 2), *A\u2192C* (weight 4), *C\u2192B* (weight \u22123).  \n\n- Initially: *dist[A]=0*, *dist[B]=\u221e*, *dist[C]=\u221e*.  \n- Relax *A*: *dist[B]=2*, *dist[C]=4*.  \n- Pick *B* (smallest tentative distance = 2), mark it settled.  \n- Relax *C\u2192B*: new path *A\u2192C\u2192B* has cost 4 + (\u22123) = 1 < 2, but *B* is already settled, so Dijkstra does **not** update *dist[B]*.  \n\nFinal *dist[B]* = 2 (incorrect; correct shortest path cost is 1).\n\n**Alternative:** Use the **Bellman\u2013Ford algorithm**, which relaxes all edges *V\u22121* times, guaranteeing correctness even with negative edges (as long as no negative cycles exist).",
